CAT = 'rbtAcq'; SEG = 'SACQ'
Q = [
('During a matching program, Leo gets a high five after every correct match for two weeks. His plan now says he should earn praise after about every third correct match, sometimes after two and sometimes after four. Which schedule is the RBT moving to?',
 'Variable ratio 3 schedule', ['Fixed ratio 3 schedule', 'Variable interval 3 schedule', 'Fixed interval 3 schedule'],
 'The number of responses needed varies around an average of three, which is a variable ratio 3 schedule. A fixed ratio 3 would require exactly three every time, and interval schedules depend on time passing.'),
('A plan says a client earns a short break after the first on-task response once each 5 minutes has passed. The RBT notices the client works very little right after each break and more near the 5-minute mark. This pattern fits:',
 'A fixed interval schedule', ['A variable ratio schedule', 'A fixed ratio schedule', 'Continuous reinforcement'],
 'A fixed interval schedule often produces a scallop: little responding after reinforcement and more as the interval ends. Ratio schedules depend on response counts, not elapsed time.'),
('Maya now completes worksheets with fewer errors, but her plan calls for a stronger reinforcer for answers she gets right with no prompt than for prompted answers. What is the RBT adjusting?',
 'Magnitude by response quality', ['Schedule by elapsed time', 'Immediacy by prompt level', 'Variety by length of the session'],
 'Giving a larger or better reinforcer for independent answers than for prompted ones is differential reinforcement by magnitude. The schedule and timing stay the same; only the amount or quality changes.'),
('A client asks for a break by handing over a break card, and the RBT lets him leave the work table for 2 minutes. Break card use goes up across sessions. This is:',
 'Negative reinforcement', ['Positive reinforcement', 'Negative punishment', 'Escape extinction'],
 'Work demands are removed after the response and the response increases, so it is negative reinforcement. Escape extinction would mean not removing the demands.'),
('A client is offered the same fruit snack after every correct response, and by the 20th trial he pushes it away. Which change is most likely to restore its effect?',
 'Rotate among several snacks', ['Give two snacks per response', 'Wait longer before giving it', 'Move to a fixed interval'],
 'Pushing the snack away suggests satiation, and varying the reinforcers is the usual fix. A bigger amount of the same snack adds to satiation, and delaying it weakens reinforcement.'),
('The RBT praises a correct answer, writes the data, checks the next program, and then hands the token about 15 seconds later. What does this most weaken?',
 'Immediacy of reinforcement', ['Magnitude of reinforcement', 'Variety of reinforcement', 'Contingency of reinforcement'],
 'Reinforcers work best delivered right after the response, so a 15-second delay weakens immediacy. The token is still given only for the correct answer, so the contingency is intact.'),
('A client does not respond to praise. The plan has the RBT say "Great job!" just before handing over a favorite toy, many times a session. What is the plan trying to do?',
 'Pair praise with a reinforcer', ['Fade the toy into praise', 'Set up a token economy', 'Give praise on a time schedule'],
 'Saying praise right before a known reinforcer, again and again, pairs it so praise becomes a conditioned reinforcer. Giving praise on a time schedule without the toy would not pair it with anything.'),
('After weeks of pairing, the RBT wants to test whether praise now works as a reinforcer for a client. Which result would show it does?',
 'A response increases with praise', ['The client smiles when praised', 'The client tolerates being praised', 'Praise is paired more often'],
 'A stimulus is a reinforcer only if behavior it follows increases. Smiling or tolerating praise does not show a change in the future rate of the behavior.'),
("To build a new token's value, one RBT hands the token and then a snack a moment later. Another RBT gives the snack first and the token after it. Why is the second order less effective?",
 'The token no longer predicts food', ['The snack loses its value', 'It is a variable schedule', 'The token becomes a generalized one'],
 'Pairing works when the neutral stimulus comes just before the reinforcer and predicts it. Giving the token after the snack means it signals nothing new.'),
("A client's tokens can be traded for snacks, tablet time, stickers, or a walk. Why do the tokens keep working even when the client has just eaten?",
 'They are generalized reinforcers', ['They are unconditioned reinforcers', 'They are on a fixed schedule', 'They are noncontingent rewards'],
 'Tokens backed by many reinforcers are generalized conditioned reinforcers, so satiation on one backup item does not end their value. They are conditioned, not unconditioned, because they were learned.'),
('In a receptive ID program, the RBT says "Touch cup," the client touches the cup, the RBT gives praise and a chip, then pauses 3 seconds before the next card. The 3-second pause is the:',
 'Intertrial interval', ['Response latency', 'Prompt delay interval', 'Duration of the response'],
 'The short pause after the consequence and before the next SD is the intertrial interval. Latency is the time from SD to response, which happens earlier in the trial.'),
('During discrete trials, the RBT says "Touch red. Touch red, buddy. Come on, touch the red one." The client touches red after the third phrase. What should the RBT change?',
 'Give the SD once, clearly', ['Give a bigger reinforcer', 'Add a model prompt first', 'Lengthen the intertrial pause'],
 'In DTT the SD is given once in a clear, consistent form. Repeating and changing the wording teaches the client to wait for extra instructions.'),
("A client answers a discrete trial incorrectly. The plan calls for an error correction: re-present the SD with a prompt, then give a transfer trial. What is the RBT's next step after the prompted correct answer?",
 'Present the SD with no prompt', ['Move to a new target card', 'Give the biggest reinforcer', 'Repeat the same prompted trial'],
 'A transfer trial presents the SD again without the prompt so the response comes under control of the SD. Moving on without it leaves the client practicing only the prompted response.'),
('In a DTT drill, the RBT puts the correct card in the same spot on every trial. The client gets 100% but fails when a supervisor mixes the order. What error made this happen?',
 'Not rotating card position', ['Using too many targets', 'Delivering praise too fast', 'Giving the SD only once'],
 'Keeping the correct card in one spot lets position, not the picture, control the response. Card positions should be rotated so the client attends to the stimulus itself.'),
('A plan asks the RBT to mix mastered and new targets during discrete trials. What is the main reason for this?',
 'To keep reinforcement rates high', ['To shorten the intertrial interval', 'To test generalization', 'To fade the prompt faster'],
 'Interspersing mastered targets gives the client frequent easy success and reinforcement, which helps motivation during harder new targets. It does not by itself test generalization across settings or people.'),
('At snack time, Sam looks at the juice on a high shelf and reaches. The RBT holds up the juice, waits, and gives it when Sam says "juice." Which procedure is this?',
 'Incidental teaching', ['Discrete-trial teaching', 'Errorless teaching', 'Backward chaining'],
 'The client starts the opportunity by showing interest, and the RBT uses that moment to prompt and reinforce the request. That is incidental teaching, a naturalistic procedure.'),
('A client is learning to request "push" on a swing. In natural environment training, which consequence fits best after he says "push"?',
 'Pushing the swing right away', ['Giving a token for later', 'Praising and giving a snack', 'Starting a new discrete trial'],
 'In natural environment training the reinforcer is directly related to the response, so saying "push" earns a push. Tokens or snacks are arbitrary reinforcers more common in DTT.'),
('Which arrangement best sets up an opportunity for a client to request "more" at snack time?',
 'Giving one small piece of snack', ['Leaving a full bowl in reach', 'Giving snack every 2 minutes', 'Naming the snack as she eats'],
 'A small portion of a preferred item leaves the client wanting more, which sets up a mand for "more." A full bowl in reach or snack on a timer removes the reason to ask, and naming the snack models a tact, not a mand.'),
('During play, a client says "car" when he wants the car. His plan targets two-word requests. Using naturalistic teaching, what should the RBT do?',
 "Prompt 'red car,' then give it", ['Hand over the car right away', 'Remove the car and run a drill', 'Wait silently for two words'],
 "Incidental teaching uses the client's request to prompt a slightly more advanced response, then gives the item. Ignoring him with no prompt risks frustration and loses the teaching moment."),
('A parent watches one session of discrete trials at the table and one of play-based teaching on the floor. Which feature most separates the play-based session?',
 "The client's interests lead", ['Each trial starts with an SD', 'Reinforcers follow responses', 'The RBT records the data'],
 "In naturalistic teaching the teaching follows the client's interests and natural routines. Both methods use antecedents, reinforcement and data, so those do not separate them."),
("A client's handwashing task analysis has 8 steps. The RBT prompts steps 1 through 7 and the client does step 8, drying hands, on his own to earn praise. Which procedure is this?",
 'Backward chaining', ['Forward chaining', 'Total task chaining', 'Shaping the last step'],
 'In backward chaining the trainer completes or prompts all steps except the last, which the client does independently and is reinforced. Forward chaining teaches the first step first.'),
('A client in forward chaining has mastered steps 1 and 2 of putting on a jacket. What should the RBT do next session?',
 'Add step 3 after steps 1 and 2', ['Teach the final step alone', 'Prompt every step equally', 'Reinforce only step 2 for now'],
 'In forward chaining the client does mastered steps and the next step is added in order, with reinforcement after the new step. Teaching the final step first would be backward chaining.'),
('A client can do most steps of brushing teeth but needs help with a few. His plan has him go through every step each session, with prompts only where needed. This is:',
 'Total task chaining', ['Backward chaining', 'Forward chaining', 'Shaping each step'],
 'Total task chaining has the client work through the whole chain each time, prompting only the steps not yet mastered. It suits clients who already do many steps.'),
('A client learning to make toast by backward chaining reaches reinforcement within seconds of each session starting. Why does backward chaining make this happen?',
 'The last step leads to it', ['Every step earns a token', 'The first step is easiest', 'Prompts are faded right away'],
 'The final step of a chain is followed by the natural outcome, such as toast ready to eat, so teaching it first puts the client right next to reinforcement. Not every step is reinforced in chaining.'),
('A client in a dressing chain does step 3 independently but skips step 4 and jumps to step 5. Per most chaining plans, what should the RBT do?',
 'Block and prompt step 4', ['Reinforce step 5 anyway', 'Start over at step 1 later', 'Skip step 4 in the data'],
 'Each step in a chain should be done in order, so the RBT prevents the skip and prompts the missed step. Reinforcing step 5 would strengthen the out-of-order chain.'),
('In a color program, the RBT reinforces "red" only when a red card is shown and gives no reinforcement when the client says "red" to a blue card. The blue card is serving as:',
 'An S-delta for that response', ['An SD for that response', 'A conditioned reinforcer', 'A stimulus prompt for blue'],
 'A stimulus in whose presence a response is not reinforced is an S-delta. The red card, signaling that "red" will be reinforced, is the SD.'),
('A client correctly touches "dog" when it is the only card on the table but guesses when a cat card is added. What should the next teaching step include?',
 'A field with distractor cards', ['More trials with one card', 'A larger reinforcer for dog', 'A longer delay before prompts'],
 'Discrimination requires responding to the target among other stimuli, so the client needs trials with distractors in the field. Practicing with a single card cannot teach the difference between cards.'),
('A client says "cookie" for cookies but also for crackers and chips. Which procedure would best teach him to say "cookie" only for cookies?',
 'Discrimination training', ['Response generalization', 'Stimulus generalization', 'Noncontingent reinforcement'],
 'Saying "cookie" to similar items is overgeneralization, and discrimination training brings the response under control of cookies alone. Stimulus generalization is the problem here, not the fix.'),
('After discrimination training, a client says "hi" when a person enters the room but not when the room is empty. This shows the response is under:',
 'Stimulus control', ['Response generalization', 'Schedule control', 'Motivating operations'],
 'A response that reliably occurs in the presence of one stimulus and not another is under stimulus control. Response generalization would be new, similar responses to the same stimulus.'),
('During a "same versus different" program, the client is correct on 90% of trials with the RBT but scores low with the parent, who uses a new phrase. What has the parent\'s phrase likely done?',
 'Changed the SD for the task', ['Removed the S-delta from it', 'Thinned the reinforcement', 'Added a stimulus prompt to it'],
 "A new phrase is a different SD, and the response is still controlled only by the RBT's wording. This points to a need for generalization across people and instructions, which the RBT should share with the supervisor."),
('For a new skill, the RBT first uses full physical guidance, then partial physical, then a gesture, and finally no prompt as the client succeeds. This is:',
 'Most-to-least prompting', ['Least-to-most prompting', 'Constant time delay', 'Stimulus fading'],
 'Starting with the most help and reducing it as the client succeeds is most-to-least prompting. Least-to-most starts with the least help and adds more only when needed.'),
('A plan says: give the SD, wait 5 seconds; if no response, give a gesture; wait again; if no response, model; then physical guidance. Which prompt procedure is this?',
 'Least-to-most prompting', ['Most-to-least prompting', 'Progressive time delay', 'Errorless teaching'],
 'The client has a chance to respond independently, and more help is added only after no response. That is least-to-most prompting.'),
('On the first sessions, the RBT gives the SD and the prompt at the same moment. Later she waits 2 seconds, then 4, before prompting. What is this procedure?',
 'Progressive time delay', ['Constant time delay', 'Least-to-most prompting', 'Stimulus fading'],
 'Starting at a zero-second delay and gradually increasing the wait before the prompt is progressive time delay. Constant time delay keeps the same wait after the zero-second trials.'),
('To teach the letter A, the RBT writes it in thick dark lines on a worksheet, then makes the lines lighter each week until only dots remain. This is:',
 'Stimulus fading', ['Response prompt fading', 'Stimulus shaping', 'Graduated guidance'],
 'Gradually changing a feature of the stimulus itself, such as how dark the lines are, is stimulus fading. Response prompts are things the RBT does, such as gestures or models.'),
('A client becomes upset after errors, so his plan arranges prompts so that he almost never answers incorrectly while learning. This approach is called:',
 'Errorless teaching', ['Least-to-most prompting', 'Error correction', 'Discrimination training'],
 'Errorless teaching uses prompts and their careful fading so the client rarely makes errors. Least-to-most allows errors before help is added, which this plan avoids.'),
('A client waits for the RBT to point before choosing any card, even on targets he mastered months ago. Which problem should the RBT describe to the supervisor?',
 'Prompt dependence', ['Response generalization', 'Satiation on praise', 'Extinction of the skill'],
 'Waiting for a prompt even on known targets is prompt dependence, a sign that prompts were not faded. The RBT should share the pattern with the supervisor so the fading plan can be changed.'),
('A client learned to wash his hands at the clinic sink with his RBT. The plan now has his parent and teacher run the same steps at home and school. What is being promoted?',
 'Generalization across settings', ['Maintenance of the skill', 'Response generalization', 'Discrimination across settings'],
 'Teaching the skill with new people and places promotes generalization across settings and people. Maintenance is about the skill lasting over time after teaching ends.'),
('To teach "dog," the RBT uses photos of many breeds, cartoon dogs and toy dogs. Why does the plan use so many examples?',
 'To promote stimulus generalization', ['To promote response generalization', 'To shape a new response', "To keep the client's attention"],
 'Teaching with multiple examples helps the client respond correctly to new members of the class, which is stimulus generalization. One photo alone could leave the response tied to that picture.'),
('A client was taught to wipe a table with a sponge. Without teaching, she starts wiping it with a paper towel and with a cloth. This is:',
 'Response generalization', ['Stimulus generalization', 'Skill maintenance', 'Stimulus control'],
 'New, untaught forms of a response that do the same job show response generalization. Stimulus generalization would be the same response (sponge wiping) to new stimuli, such as a different table.'),
('A client requests snacks well at the clinic but never at home. Which step would most help this skill generalize?',
 'Practice at home with caregivers', ['Add more trials at the clinic', 'Use a larger snack at the clinic', 'Return to full physical prompts'],
 'Generalization is promoted by practicing in the setting and with the people where the skill is needed. More clinic trials only strengthen the skill where it already occurs.'),
('A client met mastery for labeling 10 animals three weeks ago. His plan now probes them once a week on a thin schedule. What kind of procedure is this?',
 'A maintenance procedure', ['An acquisition procedure', 'A shaping procedure', 'A baseline probe'],
 'Checking mastered skills at intervals with thinner reinforcement keeps them strong over time, which is maintenance. Acquisition procedures teach new skills, usually with dense reinforcement and prompts.'),
('Which session detail most clearly marks a target as being in acquisition rather than maintenance?',
 'Frequent prompts and reinforcers', ['Probes about once a week', 'A thinned reinforcement schedule', 'Independent answers on probes'],
 'Acquisition targets are being learned, so they get frequent prompting and dense reinforcement. Weekly probes and thin schedules are typical of maintenance.'),
('The plan says that if a client misses a mastered target on a weekly maintenance probe, the target goes back into daily teaching. She misses 4 of 10 animals. What should the RBT do?',
 'Return it to teaching per the plan', ['Wait for the supervisor before acting', 'Drop the target from the plan', 'Skip the probe until next month'],
 'The plan already states the rule for a missed probe, so the RBT records the result and returns the target to teaching. Waiting for the supervisor to decide something the plan has decided delays teaching.'),
('A plan moves mastered shapes from every-trial reinforcement to praise on a variable ratio 4. Why thin the schedule during maintenance?',
 'To make the skill last longer', ['To teach a new response', 'To increase the prompt levels', 'To speed up skill acquisition'],
 'Intermittent schedules make behavior more resistant to extinction, so the skill is likelier to last when natural reinforcement is less frequent. Acquisition is when dense reinforcement is used.'),
('A client says "ba" for ball. The RBT now reinforces only "baw" or closer, then only "bal," and finally only "ball." This procedure is:',
 'Shaping the word', ['Chaining the sounds', 'Stimulus fading', 'Prompt fading'],
 'Reinforcing successive approximations of the target response while no longer reinforcing earlier forms is shaping. Chaining links different steps; shaping changes one response.'),
('During a shaping program for sitting at the table, a client sat for 20 seconds several sessions in a row. The next criterion is 30 seconds. If he now sits for 20 seconds, the RBT should:',
 'Withhold reinforcement for it', ['Reinforce it just as before', 'Give a bigger reinforcer for it', 'Prompt him to stand up'],
 'In shaping, once the criterion moves up, earlier approximations are no longer reinforced. Continuing to reinforce 20 seconds would keep the client at that level.'),
('A shaping plan raises the hand-raising criterion each day. The client stops responding and gets upset after a large jump in criterion. What likely happened?',
 'The steps moved too fast', ['Extinction was not used', 'The steps were too small', 'The reinforcer was too big'],
 'Steps that move too fast can put reinforcement out of reach, and responding drops off with frustration. Steps that are too small slow progress but do not usually stop it.'),
("A supervisor asks the RBT which of a client's goals will be taught by shaping rather than chaining. Which goal fits shaping?",
 'Saying a word more clearly', ['Putting on a button shirt', 'Washing dishes at the sink', 'Making a peanut sandwich'],
 'Shaping changes one response, such as speech clarity, toward a target. Putting on a shirt or making a sandwich are multi-step chains taught with task analyses.'),
('A client earns a token for each completed task and trades 5 tokens for tablet time. After two months, his plan raises it to 8 tokens. What is the plan doing?',
 'Thinning the token schedule', ['Adding response cost', 'Changing the backup items', 'Moving to a fixed interval'],
 'Requiring more tokens per exchange thins the schedule of backup reinforcement. Response cost would mean removing tokens after problem behavior.'),
('A new token board is introduced. The client does not yet value the tokens. What should the plan do first?',
 'Let him trade tokens right away', ['Require 10 tokens per trade', 'Take away tokens for errors', 'Give all tokens at session end'],
 'Early on, tokens are exchanged quickly, often after one token, so they become paired with backup items. Requiring many tokens before the client values them may mean he never contacts the backup.'),
('In a token program, a client is told he cannot trade for his chosen backup item because he has only 3 of 5 tokens. He picks a smaller item at 3 tokens. Which feature of good token programs is shown?',
 'A menu of priced backups', ['Response cost for errors', 'Noncontingent tokens', 'A fixed interval exchange'],
 'Good token programs offer a menu of backup reinforcers at different prices so the client can choose. Nothing was removed and the tokens were earned, so this is not response cost or noncontingent delivery.'),
('The plan says the client earns a token for each correct answer. The RBT gives a token after a correct answer and says "Nice reading!" at the same time. Why pair praise with the token?',
 'So praise gains reinforcing value', ['So the token buys more backups', 'To replace the backup items soon', 'To set up a variable schedule'],
 "Pairing praise with tokens helps praise become a conditioned reinforcer, which supports later fading of the tokens. It does not change the token's exchange value."),
]
